---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import RebaseHelper from '../../components/RebaseHelper.jsx';
---
<ToolLayout title="Git Rebase vs Merge — When to Use Each" description="Click-to-copy reference for common git rebase commands, plus a clear breakdown of when to rebase vs when to merge.">
  <RebaseHelper client:load slot="tool" />

  <h2>Git Rebase vs Merge: Which Should You Use?</h2>
  <p>Both combine changes from one branch into another, but they handle history differently. <strong>Merge</strong> creates a new commit joining two histories, preserving exactly what happened. <strong>Rebase</strong> replays your commits on top of another branch, producing a clean, linear history — but it rewrites commit hashes in the process.</p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Situation</th><th>Use</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Updating a shared/public branch</td><td>Merge</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Cleaning up your own local feature branch before a PR</td><td>Rebase</td></tr>
      <tr><td>You want to preserve exact history of when things happened</td><td>Merge</td></tr>
      <tr><td>You want a clean, linear commit log</td><td>Rebase</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Team is less experienced with git</td><td>Merge (safer default)</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>The short version: <strong>rebase locally, merge when it's shared.</strong></p>

  <h2>Why rebase requires more caution than merge</h2>
  <p>Rebase rewrites commit history, giving commits new hashes — perfectly safe for local, unpushed work, but genuinely disruptive if applied to commits others have already pulled, since their local history no longer matches and requires manual reconciliation.</p>

  <h2>How to use this reference</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Find the rebase command matching your situation (interactive, onto a branch, continuing after conflicts).</li>
    <li>Confirm the commits involved haven't been pushed and shared, unless you understand the implications.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Common Rebase Mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Rebasing already-pushed, shared commits</strong>, breaking history for anyone who's already pulled those commits and forcing them to manually reconcile.</li>
    <li><strong>Not resolving conflicts fully</strong> before running <code>rebase --continue</code>, leaving the repository in a confusing intermediate state.</li>
    <li><strong>Force-pushing after a rebase without team awareness</strong>, silently overwriting shared branch history other people are working from.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>When to Use This Rebase Reference</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Cleaning up local commit history before opening a pull request</li>
    <li>Keeping a feature branch up to date with main via rebase instead of merge</li>
    <li>Squashing several small commits into one logical commit interactively</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Git Rebase vs Merge FAQ</h2>
  <p><strong>What's the difference between rebase and merge?</strong> Merge preserves full history with an extra merge commit; rebase rewrites your commits to sit on top of the target branch, producing a linear history with no merge commit.</p>
  <p><strong>Is rebase safer than merge?</strong> Not inherently — rebase is safe on commits only you have, but risky on commits others have already pulled, since it changes commit hashes and breaks their local history.</p>
  <p><strong>When is it safe to rebase?</strong> Only on commits that exist solely in your local branch and haven't been pushed and shared with others — once shared, prefer merge or coordinate carefully with your team.</p>
</ToolLayout>
